h2("4.3. False-Alarm Control with a Validation Budget")
p("Figure 3 compares the four threshold rules on UNSW-NB15. The two conventional data-driven rules do not help. The F1-optimal threshold slightly *increases* the test FPR of every model (by 1.3–4.4 points), and Youden's threshold changes it by at most ±2.3 points. In contrast, the budget rule τ_{FPR}(1%) lowers the test FPR of every model by 18–23 points, to 4.4–6.5%, and raises F1 by 3.6–4.2 points, at the cost of 5.7–7.9 points of recall. For FTT-IDS, 8211 of 9854 false alarms (83%) are removed on average. Figure 4 locates the two operating points on the ROC and precision–recall curves. The shift is a movement along an unchanged curve; the ranking quality of the models is good (ROC-AUC ≈ 0.98), and the false alarms at τ = 0.5 come from an inappropriate operating point.")
fig("fig3_threshold_rules.png", "Effect of four validation-based threshold rules on the UNSW-NB15 test false-positive rate (left) and F1 (right). Bars show the mean over five seeds; error bars show ±1 s.d. Only the false-alarm budget rule (yellow) reduces false alarms.")
fig("fig4_roc_pr.png", "ROC (left) and precision–recall (right) curves on the UNSW-NB15 test set (seed 42). Markers show the FTT-IDS operating points at τ = 0.5 (circle) and at the 1% validation false-alarm budget (square).")
p("Table 8 shows that these conclusions do not depend on the particular value α = 1%. Any budget between 0.5% and 2% yields a test F1 of 93.2–93.7% for FTT-IDS and XGBoost, compared with 89.5% at τ = 0.5, and the trade-off between false alarms and recall is monotone. The table also explains why the default threshold performs poorly. On the validation data, τ = 0.5 corresponds to a false-positive rate of 9.6% for FTT-IDS and 7.7% for XGBoost; a budget of α = 10% reproduces the default operating point of FTT-IDS almost exactly (test FPR 27.6%, F1 89.2%). The default threshold is thus an implicit, and unusually permissive, false-alarm budget that goes unnoticed because validation accuracy remains high (93–94%). The F1-optimal and Youden rules stay close to it because, under the balanced validation class distribution, they tolerate many false alarms in exchange for a small gain in recall.")
table("Table 8. Sweep of the validation false-alarm budget α on UNSW-NB15 (mean over five seeds; %). No retraining is involved; only the threshold changes.",
      ["Budget α (validation)", "FTT-IDS test FPR", "FTT-IDS recall", "FTT-IDS F1", "XGBoost test FPR", "XGBoost recall", "XGBoost F1"],
      [["0.1", "0.30", "80.59", "89.12", "0.19", "79.68", "88.61"],
       ["0.5", "2.62", "89.17", "93.22", "3.34", "90.09", "93.44"],
       ["1", "4.44", "91.25", "93.65", "5.81", "92.12", "93.59"],
       ["2", "7.54", "93.16", "93.48", "9.83", "94.25", "93.19"],
       ["5", "16.45", "96.38", "91.88", "19.37", "97.12", "91.22"],
       ["10", "27.62", "98.67", "89.21", "30.88", "98.98", "88.30"],
       ["τ = 0.5 (for reference)", "26.63", "98.49", "89.45", "26.29", "98.37", "89.50"]],
      [2200, 1300, 1200, 1100, 1400, 1300, 1200])
p("Table 9 shows the price of the 1% budget for each attack category. Most categories keep detection rates above 94%, but Fuzzers fall from 90.2% to 48.6% and Shellcode from 99.1% to 88.8%, and XGBoost behaves in the same way. Fuzzing traffic is the attack family whose flow statistics are closest to benign traffic. A single binary threshold therefore cannot serve all attack families equally, and an operator for whom fuzzing matters would have to accept a larger budget (Table 8) or add a dedicated detector. On TON_IoT, the validation FPR of most models is already close to 1% at τ = 0.5, and the budget rule changes little: FTT-IDS moves from an FPR of 1.58% to 1.06% and loses 0.1 F1 points. The rule is useful when the default operating point is badly placed, as on UNSW-NB15, and neutral otherwise.")
