h2("4.6. Explanation Reliability")
p("**Global explanations.** Figure 6 shows the global SHAP importance of FTT-IDS on both datasets. On UNSW-NB15, the explanations are dominated by three TTL-derived features (*ct_state_ttl*, *sttl* and *dttl*), followed by *service* and *proto*. This agrees with the single-seed ranking of the MSc thesis (*sttl*, *ct_state_ttl*, *state*) but is more concentrated on TTL information. On TON_IoT, packet and byte counts (*dst_pkts*, *src_pkts*, *dst_bytes*), the connection state and the duration dominate. The relative additivity error of the expected-gradients estimates was 13–17% on UNSW-NB15 and 13–19% on TON_IoT. Individual attributions are therefore approximate, and the analysis below relies on aggregates over records and seeds.")
fig("fig_global_shap.png", "Global SHAP importance of FTT-IDS on UNSW-NB15 (left) and TON_IoT (right): mean absolute SHAP value of the attack margin over 2000 test records, averaged over five seeds (error bars: s.d. across seeds).")
p("**Stability.** Table 12 summarises the stability results. For a fixed model, explanations were almost insensitive to the choice of background sample (Spearman ρ ≥ 0.99). Across independently trained models, stability was high on TON_IoT (ρ = 0.95) but only moderate on UNSW-NB15 (ρ = 0.76, Kendall τ = 0.60), and the top-10 feature sets of two seeds had a Jaccard overlap of only 0.57, i.e., about seven common features. The MSc model (A1) behaved similarly (ρ = 0.75). The large error bars in Figure 6 show the same effect: two models with practically identical accuracy can distribute importance differently among correlated TTL features. A single-seed SHAP ranking, as reported in most XAI-NIDS studies, is therefore one draw from a distribution of plausible explanations.")
table("Table 12. Reliability of FTT-IDS explanations (mean ± s.d.). Faithfulness: test ROC-AUC after masking the k most important vs. k random features (no retraining).",
      ["Property", "Statistic", "UNSW-NB15", "TON_IoT"],
      [["Stability across backgrounds", "Spearman ρ / top-10 Jaccard", "0.998 / 0.91", "0.990 / 0.82"],
       ["Stability across 5 seeds (10 pairs)", "Spearman ρ", "0.764 ± 0.062", "0.949 ± 0.015"],
       ["", "Kendall τ", "0.597 ± 0.058", "0.828 ± 0.026"],
       ["", "Top-10 Jaccard", "0.568 ± 0.095", "0.727 ± 0.078"],
       ["Agreement with permutation importance", "Spearman ρ / top-10 overlap", "0.760 / 7 of 10", "0.861 / 8 of 10"],
       ["Faithfulness, k = 5", "AUC: SHAP top-k vs. random k", "0.738 vs. 0.980", "0.824 vs. 0.971"],
       ["Faithfulness, k = 10", "AUC: SHAP top-k vs. random k", "0.746 vs. 0.922", "0.648 vs. 0.934"],
       ["Local deletion, k = 5 (seed 42)", "Alert flip rate: SHAP vs. random", "7.5% vs. 2.6%", "0.3% vs. 0.6%"]],
      [2900, 2700, 1850, 1850])
p("**Faithfulness.** Figure 7 shows that masking the globally most important features degrades the model far more than masking random features. On UNSW-NB15, masking the top five features lowers ROC-AUC from 0.984 to 0.738, while masking five random features leaves it at 0.980; on TON_IoT the corresponding values are 0.824 and 0.971. The global rankings therefore identify features on which the trained model depends. Two qualifications apply. First, the curve is not monotone on UNSW-NB15 (0.738 for *k* = 5 and 0.746 for *k* = 10) and its variance across seeds is large (s.d. 0.09–0.10), so the ranking is informative for the leading features but not for the order of the features below them. Second, the evidence at the level of individual alerts is weak: masking an alert's own top five features flipped 7.5% of UNSW-NB15 alerts (2.6% for random features) and lowered the attack probability by 0.137 (0.015 for random features), and on TON_IoT local deletion had no measurable effect, because alerts are supported by many redundant features. The SHAP values are thus suitable for ranking features globally and for comparing groups of records, but they do not identify a small set of features that is decisive for a single alert. Permutation importance, a model-agnostic method, agreed with SHAP on seven (UNSW-NB15) and eight (TON_IoT) of the top-10 features.")
fig("fig_faithfulness.png", "Faithfulness of global SHAP rankings: test ROC-AUC of FTT-IDS after replacing the k most important features (blue) or k random features (orange) by reference values, without retraining (mean ± s.d. over five seeds; random masks also averaged over five draws).")

h2("4.7. What Explains the False Alarms?")
p("Figure 8 shows the mean SHAP profile of each prediction outcome on UNSW-NB15. False positives look like true positives in the TTL features: *ct_state_ttl* and *sttl* push false alarms towards the attack class (+2.46 and +2.16) almost as strongly as genuine attacks (+3.25 and +3.01), whereas these features push true negatives firmly towards normal (−2.42 and −2.26). For 56% of the explained false alarms, *sttl* is the single largest contributor. The non-TTL features (for example *service* and *proto*) instead point weakly towards normal in false alarms. The model raises these alerts because of their TTL values.")
fig("fig_shap_by_outcome.png", "Mean SHAP value of the ten globally most important features for true positives (TP), false negatives (FN), false positives (FP) and true negatives (TN) of FTT-IDS on UNSW-NB15 (seed 42, validation-F1 threshold; up to 400 records per group). Red pushes towards an alert, blue towards normal.")
p("Figure 9 shows the data behind this behaviour. In the de-duplicated training data, 75% of normal flows have *sttl* = 31 and only 19% have *sttl* = 254, whereas 75% of attacks have *sttl* = 254. In the official test set, the share of normal flows with *sttl* = 254 doubles to 41%. Of the false alarms of FTT-IDS at τ = 0.5, 88% are normal flows with *sttl* = 254, most of them with state FIN (73%) and no identified service (83%). At the budget threshold, the share is still 80%.")
fig("fig_sttl_shift.png", "Distribution of the source TTL (sttl) by partition and class in UNSW-NB15 (training data after de-duplication; official test set). Normal test traffic contains a much larger share of sttl = 254 flows than normal training traffic.")
p("A natural remedy would be to remove the TTL features. To test this, FTT-IDS and XGBoost were retrained with five seeds without *sttl* and *ct_state_ttl*, and again without all three TTL features (*sttl*, *dttl* and *ct_state_ttl*). Neither intervention reduced false alarms. Without any TTL feature, FTT-IDS reached F1 89.77% and FPR 24.98% at τ = 0.5 (93.17% and 4.85% at the budget threshold); XGBoost reached 89.29% and 26.45% (93.21% and 5.86%). Moreover, the false alarms of the TTL-free models are largely the *same flows*. The Jaccard overlap between the false-alarm sets of the full and the TTL-free FTT-IDS was 0.77 on average over seeds, and 88% of the TTL-free model's false alarms still have *sttl* = 254, even though the model never saw this feature. The TTL fields are therefore not the cause of the errors but the most legible proxy for a sub-population of benign test traffic that is under-represented among normal training flows. Without them, the model recognises the same population through correlated features. SHAP correctly points to the proxy; feature removal does not fix the underlying shift. Interventions on the decision, namely the budget threshold and its recalibration on target traffic (Section 4.4), remain the effective controls.")

h2("4.8. Model Size and Inference Cost")
p("FTT-IDS has 577,410 trainable parameters on UNSW-NB15; most of them belong to the 2048-wide feed-forward blocks inherited from the MSc configuration. FT-Transformer has 937,538 parameters, the MLP 117,858 and the 1D-CNN 178,946. On a laptop CPU, scoring a single flow took a median of 1.43 ms with FTT-IDS, compared with 2.49 ms with FT-Transformer and 0.31 ms with the MLP and the 1D-CNN. On the GPU, FTT-IDS scored about 95,000 flows per second in batches of 1024. No claim of a lightweight design is made. These figures only indicate that per-flow scoring is not a bottleneck for offline or near-real-time analysis on commodity hardware.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5
h1("5. Discussion")
p("**Accuracy is not the differentiator.** Under a protocol that removes duplicate-induced leakage and gives every model a comparable tuning budget, the feature-token Transformer matches but does not beat gradient-boosted trees on UNSW-NB15, at fixed thresholds as well as at matched false-alarm rates, and it trails them slightly on TON_IoT. This result is consistent with tabular-learning benchmarks {grinsztajn,shwartz}. It also suggests that some of the near-perfect accuracies reported for Transformer-based NIDSs may owe more to evaluation choices than to architecture {arp}. Within the feature-token family, the embedding design made no consistent difference, and the configuration carried forward from the MSc thesis performed as well as the standard one. For practitioners, the choice between a Transformer and a tree ensemble can therefore rest on other criteria, such as the availability of gradient-based explanations, integration into end-to-end pipelines or latency.")
p("**Operating points matter more than models, and the default threshold is a hidden choice.** The largest effect in this study comes from the decision rule. Changing the model alters F1 by less than one point; moving from τ = 0.5 to a 1% validation budget removes over 80% of the false alarms of every model and raises F1 by about four points. Thresholding a score at a false-alarm budget is standard practice in detection theory and is not presented here as a new method. What the analysis adds is evidence about how the common rules behave on a widely used benchmark: the default threshold corresponds to a validation false-alarm rate of 8–10%, the F1-optimal and Youden rules inherit this permissiveness, and all three therefore place the detector at an operating point that few analysts would choose deliberately. Reporting results only at τ = 0.5 or as ROC-AUC hides this degree of freedom. NIDS studies should state the decision rule explicitly and report the FPR at a fixed, operationally motivated operating point.")
p("**A validation budget is a control knob, not a guarantee.** Under the train–test shift of UNSW-NB15, the realised false-alarm rate was several times the validation budget for every model and every budget (three to five times for FTT-IDS), and most of the excess arose within benign sub-populations, where it cannot be corrected by re-weighting. A threshold derived from about 1000 benign flows of the target network restored the budget without attack labels or retraining. This points to a simple deployment recommendation: a detector trained on a benchmark should not be shipped with its training-time threshold; the threshold should be set, and periodically refreshed, from a short window of recent traffic that is believed to be attack-free. The recalibration experiment uses benign flows drawn at random from the test distribution, which is an optimistic stand-in for such a window; contamination of the window with undetected attacks and drift between recalibrations {pendlebury} would both increase the realised error and were not studied here.")
p("**Explanations need their own evaluation.** The global SHAP rankings of FTT-IDS passed the faithfulness and model-agnostic agreement checks, which supports their use for feature-level analysis. They were nevertheless only moderately stable across retrained models on UNSW-NB15, a finding that matches the retraining-stability results reported for tree models by Maseno et al. {maseno}, and they were weakly faithful at the level of single alerts. Analysts who receive a single-model explanation should not over-interpret the precise ranking of correlated features or treat the top-ranked features of one alert as its cause; aggregating explanations over several models, as done here, gives a more reliable picture. The most useful application of SHAP in this study was diagnostic. Contrasting the explanations of true and false positives revealed that false alarms are driven by TTL values, which in turn exposed a train–test shift in the benchmark. This is the type of shortcut that the XAI literature warns about {geirhos,lapuschkin}. The study also illustrates a limit of explanations: identifying the proxy feature did not make the problem removable at the feature level.")
p("**Limitations.** First, only two datasets were used, both produced by the same research group and both with flow features, and TON_IoT is close to saturation; conclusions may differ for packet-level models and for datasets such as CICIoT2023 or Edge-IIoTset {neto,ferrag}, and the shift analysis rests on UNSW-NB15 alone. Second, the study addresses binary detection; the per-category analysis covers multi-class behaviour only indirectly. Third, the hyperparameter grids were deliberately small and equal in size; larger searches could change the ranking of close competitors, although they are unlikely to change the effect of the decision rule. Fourth, the validation set served for early stopping, hyperparameter selection and threshold setting, and seed 42 was used for tuning as well as for one of the five reported runs; both introduce a small optimistic bias that affects all models alike. Fifth, the effect of removing training duplicates was not isolated by a run without de-duplication. Sixth, the explanation audit used a single attribution method whose relative additivity error was 13–19%, and its faithfulness test masked features without retraining, so part of the measured degradation may be due to out-of-distribution inputs {hooker}. Finally, the recalibration experiment assumes access to benign traffic from the target distribution, as discussed above.")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6
h1("6. Conclusions")
p("This study re-evaluated a SHAP-explained feature-token Transformer for network intrusion detection under a protocol designed to avoid common evaluation pitfalls: duplicate removal before splitting, validation-only model and threshold selection, five seeds with paired tests, comparison at matched false-alarm rates, a test-set sensitivity analysis and an audit of explanation reliability. On UNSW-NB15 and TON_IoT, the Transformer was competitive with, but not superior to, tuned gradient-boosted trees, and the design of its feature embeddings made no consistent difference. The decision rule had a far larger effect than the model. A threshold fixed at a 1% validation false-alarm budget lowered the UNSW-NB15 test false-positive rate from 26.6% to 4.4% and raised F1 from 89.5% to 93.7% for every model and on de-duplicated test data, whereas the default, F1-optimal and Youden thresholds all operated at a false-alarm rate above 24%. Because of a train–test shift, the realised false-alarm rate was several times the validation budget (4.4% instead of 1% for the Transformer); recalibrating the threshold on about 1000 benign flows from the target distribution restored the budget at 86% recall. SHAP explanations were faithful at the global level but only moderately stable across retrained models and weakly faithful for single alerts. They showed that false alarms are driven by TTL features acting as proxies for the shift, and removing those features did not remove it. Future work will extend the protocol to multi-class detection and to further IoT datasets with temporal splits, study recalibration under drift and with contaminated benign windows, and compare attribution methods under retraining-based faithfulness tests.")

